[recv] {"type": "message_start", "message": {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_GOzP06yHfCjO5joMNyiVoUTW|fc_019f4d49046b2ec1016a87952d93dc87d0a86c24b40b137c71", "toolName": "bash", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "--- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/spring-projects/spring-shell/v4.0.2/spring-shell-docs/modules/ROOT/pages/index.adoc\n= Spring Shell\nEric Bottard; Janne Valkealahti; Jay Bryant; Corneil du Plessis\n:page-section-summary-toc: 1\n\n**{project-version}**\n\nNot all applications need a fancy web user interface. Sometimes, interacting with an application through an interactive terminal is the most appropriate way to get things done.\n\nSpring Shell lets you create such a runnable application, where the user enters textual commands that are run until the program terminates. The Spring Shell project provides the infrastructure to create such a REPL (Read, Eval, Print Loop) application, letting you concentrate on implementing commands by using the familiar Spring programming model.\n\nSpring Shell includes advanced features (such as parsing, tab completion, colorization of output, fancy ASCII-art table display, input conversion, and validation), freeing you to focus on core command logic.\n\n--- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/spring-projects/spring-shell/v4.0.2/spring-shell-docs/modules/ROOT/pages/customization/jline.adoc\n--- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/spring-projects/spring-shell/v4.0.2/spring-shell-docs/modules/ROOT/pages/components.adoc\n"}], "isError": false, "timestamp": 1787270447495}}
[recv] {"type": "message_end", "message": {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_GOzP06yHfCjO5joMNyiVoUTW|fc_019f4d49046b2ec1016a87952d93dc87d0a86c24b40b137c71", "toolName": "bash", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "--- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/spring-projects/spring-shell/v4.0.2/spring-shell-docs/modules/ROOT/pages/index.adoc\n= Spring Shell\nEric Bottard; Janne Valkealahti; Jay Bryant; Corneil du Plessis\n:page-section-summary-toc: 1\n\n**{project-version}**\n\nNot all applications need a fancy web user interface. Sometimes, interacting with an application through an interactive terminal is the most appropriate way to get things done.\n\nSpring Shell lets you create such a runnable application, where the user enters textual commands that are run until the program terminates. The Spring Shell project provides the infrastructure to create such a REPL (Read, Eval, Print Loop) application, letting you concentrate on implementing commands by using the familiar Spring programming model.\n\nSpring Shell includes advanced features (such as parsing, tab completion, colorization of output, fancy ASCII-art table display, input conversion, and validation), freeing you to focus on core command logic.\n\n--- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/spring-projects/spring-shell/v4.0.2/spring-shell-docs/modules/ROOT/pages/customization/jline.adoc\n--- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/spring-projects/spring-shell/v4.0.2/spring-shell-docs/modules/ROOT/pages/components.adoc\n"}], "isError": false, "timestamp": 1787270447495}}
